  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows the U.P. under mostly sunny skies this \nafternoon as high pressure ridging continues to strengthen, with \nguidance suggesting it reaches its zenith at around 1023mb this \nevening over Lake Superior. Because of the high pressure ridge, \nhighs get into the mid-70s near Lake Superior today while areas \nfurther inland and away from the lake get into the lower 80s. This \nis reinforced by the latest area observations, which show \ntemperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s along the Lake Superior \nshoreline whereas some spots in the interior are already reaching \ninto the lower 80s.\n\nWith the high pressure retreating late tonight and three shortwave \nlows (one over the Dakotas and the other two currently over the \nCentral Plains and Colorado Rockies) lifting ENE tonight through \nthis weekend, expect cloud cover to increase by Saturday morning. \nHowever, with the shortwave over the Dakotas moving into northern \nOntario and eventually Lake Superior this weekend while the other \ntwo southerly shortwaves ride along the warm and cold fronts, \nthinking rain chances and amounts will be lower than previously \nexpected earlier this week, with the latest HREF showing most of the \narea having a 0% chance of receiving greater than a quarter inch of \nrainfall by Sunday morning; the exceptions are generally along Lake \nSuperior where slight better forcing is, squeaking chances up to 10 \nto 50%. With the southerly shortwaves depriving us of Gulf moisture, \nconvective potential is also significantly lower; currently thinking \nthe NBM is correct to have thunder chances at around a 15 to 20% \nchance, although the thunderstorm chances may begin Saturday \nafternoon over the west and south central instead of during the \nevening hours. Nevertheless, feel like the NBM forecast is close \nenough for thunder chances, so decided to roll with them for the \nofficial forecast. As the northerly shortwave rolls towards James \nBay on Sunday, temperatures aloft could be cold enough to create \nlight lake effect rainfall over the western U.P., particularly \nduring the morning hours behind the strongest cold air advection. \nHowever, as high pressure starts building back in late this weekend \ninto early next week, expect the lake effect rain showers (should \nthey occur) to be done by Sunday evening.\n\nWhile the differing model suites diverge next week, warmer \ntemperatures and drier weather is expected for Monday as shortwave \nridging and warm air advection move into the area. Behind it, \nguidance does hint at either a shortwave or front moving through the \narea sometime around mid next week. However, details are still \nhighly uncertain at this point, as the track of the shortwave could \nsignificantly alter the rainfall amounts and chances over us as the \ncold front moves through during the middle of next week. \nNevertheless, the latest NBM has wetting rainfall (greater than a \nquarter inch) chances at around a 25 to 65% between Tuesday morning \nand Wednesday morning. More high pressure ridging could be seen once \nagain over the area as we head towards the end of next week.",
